Add Ruby 4.0.6 + YJIT campaign (the deployment configuration)

Customer feedback: YJIT is enabled in production, the workload is
ruby-bench headline, and of its 16 benchmarks lobsters matters most.
Ruby moved to 4.0.6 and ruby-bench to HEAD (2c3e549 -> 13e09f7).

All eight FDO arms retrained under --yjit: enabling YJIT needs no
rebuild (rustc was present at configure time, --yjit is a runtime flag)
but it invalidates interpreter-trained profiles, because the bytecode
dispatch loop that dominated them disappears into JIT-generated code.

Result (mirrored, 16 benchmarks, spread <=0.85%):
  afdoltob +20.42% geomean, +13.77% on lobsters (715.7 -> 629.1 ms)
  pgoltob  +17.60% geomean, +9.00% on lobsters
Every arm positive on every benchmark.

On lobsters the arm ordering differs from the geomean: LTO HURTS the PGO
path (pgolto +3.92% worse than pgo +6.55%; pgob +9.71% beats pgoltob
+9.00%) while helping the AutoFDO path. afdoltob wins either way.

The mechanism changes with YJIT. Interpreter mode won by fixing dispatch
loop layout: I-cache stalls -45%, front-end bound -6.31 pp, instructions
only -1.83%. Under YJIT both arms sit at ~51-52% front-end bound and FDO
does not move it -- the front end is dominated by JIT code gcc never
sees -- so the win comes from retiring 8.85% fewer C-side instructions.
vm_exec_core falls from 31.9% of cycles to 0.84%.

NOT attributable to YJIT alone: four variables moved between campaigns
(Ruby version, YJIT, training iterations 1+2 -> 3+5, ruby-bench rev).
The clean experiment, same binaries without --yjit, is not yet run.

Also recorded: lobsters dies at 128-way concurrency in BOTH arms (base
88 HTTP 500s, afdoltob 76) from an ActiveSupport FileStore ENOENT, so it
is not a miscompilation; perf characterisation uses railsbench instead.

Methodology: measurement warmup 5 -> 12 (YJIT compiles lazily), training
1+2 -> 3+5 (JIT code is unsymbolised, so create_gcov and perf2bolt
discard those samples). Host addresses scrubbed.
